Wrangling Example

Goal: Compare Agencies Ridership over time

. . .

summary &lt;- ca_transit|&gt;
  group_by(Agency, Year)|&gt;
  summarise(tot_pass = sum(Unlinked.Passenger.Trips), .groups = "drop")|&gt;
  arrange(desc(tot_pass))

. . .
What does summary look like?

Visualizing with ggplot2

Let’s look at the agencies with the top 5 tot_pass

. . .

top5 &lt;- summary|&gt;
  filter(Year == 2023)|&gt;
  slice_max(tot_pass, n = 10)|&gt;
  pull(Agency)

summary |&gt;
  filter(Agency %in% top5) |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = Year, y = tot_pass, color = Agency)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = .6) +
  labs(title = "Transit Ridership Over Time (Top 5 Agencies)",
       y = "Persons",
       x = "Year")

summary |&gt; 
  filter(Agency == "Pomona Valley Transportation Authority") |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = Year, y = tot_pass)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.6) +
  labs(
    title = "Pomona Valley Transportation Authority Ridership Over Time",
    y = "Riders/(pop.)",
    x = "Year"
  )

group_by() and summarise(): Summariing by Category
group_by() lets us group the data by a specific variable.

cubes |&gt; 
  group_by(yellow)|&gt; 
  summarize(count = n())

group_by() and summarize(): summarizing by category
group_by() holds the rows in groups, specified by a particular variable.
summarize() collapses the rows so that there is only one row per group. A new variable is created to be the summary value.

cubes |&gt; 
  group_by(yellow) |&gt; 
  summarize(count = n())
